<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaius Poppaeus Sabinus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 35 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaius Poppaeus Sabinus was a Roman senator, consul in 9 CE, who governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a> for about twenty-four years under Augustus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>, also holding Macedonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>. In 26 CE he crushed a revolt of Thracian mountain tribes against conscription into the Roman army, as described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>, and received triumphal honours. He was the grandfather of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poppaea-sabina">Poppaea Sabina</a>, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>.</p></div>
